 ],
 "literal": "Blooming flower of tumpai, beautiful broad chest, / thick and long strong arms, for my young lord, / two arose indeed, enmities! One is, / flower-like kohl-eyes turning pale, shoulders thinning, / binding the women who looked at him; another is, / even if there is no festival, the cooked food never failing, / with relatives who eat goat meat, in the ghat water, / 'Will the elephant seize us?' saying, / hating the dwelling, the town he goes to!",
 "translation": "For my young lord, who has a beautiful broad chest adorned with blooming tumpai flowers and thick, long, strong arms, two kinds of enmities have arisen! One is that he binds the hearts of women who look at him, making their flower-like kohl-lined eyes turn pale and their shoulders grow thin. The other is that in the towns he marches against, even when there is no festival, food is never lacking; yet, his relatives who feast on goat meat look at the deep water-ghat and ask, 'Will the war-elephant seize us?', and hate staying in their dwellings.",
 "modern": "மலர்ந்த தும்பைப் பூவை அணிந்த அழகிய அகன்ற மார்பையும், திரண்ட நீண்ட வலிய கைகளையும் உடைய என் இளைய தலைவனுக்கு இரண்டு வகையான பகைகள் தோன்றியுள்ளன. ஒன்று, அவனைக் கண்ட பெண்களின் பூப்போன்ற மை தீட்டிய கண்கள் பசலை கொள்ளவும், தோள்கள் மெலியவும் செய்து அவர்களைத் தன்பால் பிணித்துக் கொள்வது. மற்றொன்று, அவன் படையெடுத்துச் செல்லும் ஊர்களில், விழாக்கள் இல்லாத காலத்திலும் உணவு தடையின்றிப் பங்கிடப்படுகிறது; ஆயினும், ஆட்டு இறைச்சியை உண்ணும் அவனது சுற்றத்தினர், நீர்நிலைகளில் உள்ள யானை தம்மைக் கொள்ளுமோ என்று அஞ்சி, அங்குத் தங்குவதை வெறுக்கின்றனர்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Avvaiyār is praising the young prince Pokuṭṭeḻiṉi, the son of Atiyamāṉ. She poetically describes two kinds of 'enmities' or troubles that arise wherever the young prince goes. The first trouble is romantic: the local women who look upon his handsome form fall deeply in love, causing them to pine away until their eyes turn pale and their shoulders grow thin. The second trouble is military: when he invades enemy territories, his relatives and soldiers feast abundantly on meat, yet they live in constant fear of his formidable war-elephants bathing in the local water-ghats, making them dread staying in those captured towns. This contrast between the soft pining of the women and the terrifying presence of the war-elephants highlights both the prince's attractive youth and his military might, fitting the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}